  nonce: fbd7  # fbd7
  data:  # fbd7
    kind: work_item  # fbd7
    title: "Give tracker-recover-disabled a HOLDER instead of a flag (fcntl lock), so it cannot leak"  # fbd7
    status: todo_hard  # fbd7
    priority: 3  # fbd7
    parent:  # fbd7
    tags:  # fbd7
    - tracker  # fbd7
    - durability  # fbd7
    isbefore: []  # fbd7
    duplicate_of: []  # fbd7
    not_duplicate_of: []  # fbd7
    pr_ref:  # fbd7
    description: "THE MARKER IS A FLAG WHERE ITS SIBLING IS A LOCK, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE BUG.\n\n`.git/tracker-recover-disabled` is a bare `touch`ed file. Nothing holds it, so\nnothing can be asked whether it is still owned, so a hard kill leaves it behind\nwith self-healing switched off and no way to tell that from a legitimate\nlong-running auto-pr.\n\nThis project already solved exactly this, for the marker next door. WI-nutin\nconverted `.git/TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING` from a marker-file-existence pattern into\nan fcntl lock, and SyncGate's docstring states the reason outright: \"The OS\nreleases the flock when the holding process exits -- even on SIGKILL -- so the\ngate cannot leak across process lifetimes.\" auto-pr already peeks that lock with\n`flock --shared --nonblock` (`_autopr_sync_gate_held`), reports the holder PID /\nalive state / lock age, and cleans up when no holder remains. The idiom is\nshipped, proven and in the same file that sets the marker being proposed here.\n\nWHAT CHANGES. Three call sites hold the marker for the life of their own\nprocess instead of touching a flag:\n  * `scripts/auto-pr` -- `exec 9>\"$REPO_ROOT/.git/tracker-recover-disabled\"`\n    plus `flock -x 9` at each action's start, replacing the three `touch` calls\n    at lines 1088 / 1497 / 2329. The EXIT-trap `rm -f` at line 380 stays (it\n    keeps the tree tidy on a clean exit); it is simply no longer load-bearing.\n  * `sync.py::do_sync` -- the same, around the span it already guards. Note its\n    `recover_marker_created` guard against clobbering an outer auto-pr's marker\n    becomes unnecessary: two holders of the same flock is exactly what a lock\n    expresses, and the inner one releasing does not free the outer.\n  * `.githooks/reference-transaction` and `.githooks/post-checkout` -- skip\n    recover when the lock is HELD rather than when the file EXISTS. A leftover\n    file with no live holder then means nothing, which is the point.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT AN AUTO-DELETE TIMER. WI-pohir ruled out a marker that clears\nitself on a timer, correctly: it would race a genuinely long auto-pr and re-open\nthe fight the marker prevents (the fetch restores a journalled op as untracked,\nthen `merge --ff-only` re-fires the hook and aborts the fast-forward that would\nhave committed it). A lock is not a timer. It is held for exactly as long as the\nholder lives, so there is no window to race -- and the auto-pr run it must not\ninterrupt can legitimately last ~45 minutes (2400s CI poll default at\n`scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh:623`, plus a 300s soft-retry), which is precisely\nwhy no wall-clock rule can do this job.\n\nA SUBTLETY TO VERIFY WHEN IMPLEMENTING, not assumed here: bash holds the lock\nthrough an inherited fd, and git subprocesses -- including the hooks themselves\n-- inherit it. A hook peeking with a FRESH `open()` takes a different open file\ndescription and so correctly observes the conflict, but that must be tested\nrather than reasoned about, in both hooks, with a live auto-pr and with a killed\none.\n\nSCOPE / GOVERNANCE. `scripts/auto-pr` and `.githooks/**` are governance files\nper AGENTS.md, so this needs explicit human approval and its own PR. That is the\nonly reason it was not bundled with the detector in PR #972; the detector is\ntracker-package-only and shipped on its own.\n\nPRIORITY RATIONALE. P3 rather than P2 because #972 already collapses the\nobservable harm: an unowned marker now announces itself on the next tracker\ncommand, which in practice is minutes rather than the ten hours observed. This\nitem removes the window instead of reporting it, which is better, but it is no\nlonger the thing standing between a leak and someone noticing."  # fbd7
    fields: {}  # fbd7
