- op: discuss  # 5e7c
  at: '2026-09-15T21:23:34Z'  # 5e7c
  by: agent  # 5e7c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5e7c
  clock: 3  # 5e7c
  nonce: 5e7c  # 5e7c
  message: "GOVERNANCE APPROVAL REQUESTED -- not implemented, no PR opened, per AGENTS.md.\n\nWHAT I WANT TO CHANGE. `scripts/auto-pr` (three `touch` sites and the EXIT-trap\n`rm`), `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/sync.py::do_sync`,\nand BOTH `.githooks/reference-transaction` and `.githooks/post-checkout`. Two of\nthose four are governance files, so the whole change needs your word first.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH TOUCHING THEM. PR #972 made a leaked marker announce itself, so\na ten-hour blind window becomes minutes. It did not stop the marker leaking. The\nleak is possible only because the marker has no holder -- and the fix for that\nis already in this repo, applied to the marker sitting next to it: WI-nutin\nturned `.git/TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING` into an fcntl lock, and SyncGate's docstring\nsays why in as many words -- the OS releases a flock even on SIGKILL, so the\ngate cannot leak across process lifetimes. auto-pr already peeks that lock and\nreports holder PID, alive state and age. I would be applying a shipped, proven\nidiom from one file to the file beside it, not inventing a mechanism.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT THE THING WI-pohir RULED OUT. That item forbade a marker that\nclears itself on a timer, because a timer races a long auto-pr and re-opens the\nvery fight the marker prevents. A lock is not a timer: it is held for exactly as\nlong as the holder lives, so there is no window to race. That matters here\nbecause the legitimate hold can reach ~45 minutes (2400s CI poll default plus a\n300s soft-retry), which is why no wall-clock rule can do this job.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT SURE OF, stated as a risk and not waved away. bash would hold the\nlock through an inherited fd, and git subprocesses -- including the hooks\nthemselves -- inherit it. I expect a hook peeking with a fresh `open()` to take\na different open file description and correctly observe the conflict, but that\nis reasoning, not evidence, and it is the kind of reasoning that is wrong often\nenough to matter. I would verify it in a throwaway repo against BOTH hooks, with\na live auto-pr and with a SIGKILLed one, BEFORE proposing a diff. If it does not\nhold, the honest outcome is that this item closes wont_do and the detector from\n#972 is the whole answer.\n\nWHAT I NEED FROM YOU. Either (a) approve the direction and I will do the\nverification and open a governance PR for your review, or (b) say the detector\nis enough and I will close this. No work proceeds on it either way until you\nanswer -- it is P3 and nothing is blocked behind it."  # 5e7c
