  message: "PRECEDENT, AND A PRIORITY QUESTION FOR YOU. The sibling item is WI-nutin:\n\"TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING marker can leak on SIGKILL -- needs fcntl.flock +\nstale-marker auto-recovery + vociferous failure messaging\". Status done,\npriority P1. That is this item's title with one noun changed.\n\nI filed this at P3 on the argument that PR #972 already collapses the observable\nharm -- a leak now announces itself on the next tracker command instead of\nsitting unseen for ten hours -- so this removes a window rather than a blind\nspot. But the identical defect in the marker next door was rated P1, and I would\nrather put the discrepancy in front of you than quietly split the difference.\nPriority is yours."  # bf36
- op: discuss  # 78e1
  at: '2026-09-15T21:57:09Z'  # 78e1
  by: agent  # 78e1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 78e1
  clock: 5  # 78e1
  nonce: '78e1'  # 78e1
  message: "VERIFICATION DONE BEFORE ANY DIFF, as this item required. The premise holds, the\nnamed risk is REAL, and it changes the design. Two of my harnesses were wrong\nfirst and said the opposite -- recorded below, because the wrong ones were the\nplausible-looking ones.\n\nTHE FOUR CASES, on a bash holder taking `exec 9>marker; flock -x 9`:\n\n  A. holder SIGKILLed, no forked children      -> lock FREE immediately.  PASS\n  B. holder SIGKILLed, a child had inherited   -> lock STILL HELD until the\n     the lock fd                                  child exits.            RISK\n  C. the hook peeks with a fresh open() while\n     running as a DESCENDANT of the holder     -> correctly sees HELD.    PASS\n  D. same as B but the fd is bash-allocated\n     via `exec {var}>` (hoping for CLOEXEC)    -> child still inherits it.\n                                                  /proc/<child>/fd/10 -> the\n                                                  marker. Mitigation FAILS.\n\nC is the concern I flagged when filing, and it is resolved: inheriting the fd\ndoes not blind the hook, because `flock --shared --nonblock` opens a fresh file\ndescription and so conflicts with the holder's exclusive lock.\n\nB AND D ARE THE FINDING. Every git and curl auto-pr forks inherits the lock fd,\nand bash offers no close-on-exec escape for it. So a SIGKILLed auto-pr does NOT\nrelease the lock until its orphaned child exits. Usually that is seconds and the\nthing self-heals, which is still strictly better than today. The bad case is an\norphaned child that HANGS: the lock stays held forever, and -- worse than today\n-- it reads as legitimately OWNED, so the PR #972 detector goes quiet. A naive\nlock conversion would trade a loud 15-minute window for a silent permanent one.\n\nSO THE DESIGN GAINS A PIECE, AND AGAIN IT IS ONE THE SIBLING ALREADY HAS. The\nlock body records `pid` and `started`, exactly as SyncGate does, and\n`_format_gate_holder_message` already renders holder pid / alive-dead / age for\nTRACKER_SYNC_PENDING. The detector then asks a FACT instead of a clock: lock\nheld AND the recorded pid is gone => an orphaned child is holding it, report\nthat. Lock held AND the pid is alive => a real run, silent. File present but\nlock free => harmless, because the hooks will no longer consult the file's\nexistence.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR PR #976, SHIPPED TWO HOURS AGO: its measured 900s grace becomes\nDEAD CODE and should be deleted with this change. The grace only existed because\nthe marker had no holder; once it has one, \"is anybody there\" stops being a\nquestion about wall-clock time. I would rather delete it than keep a constant\nthat has to be re-measured every time auto-pr's phases move.\n\nMY OWN INSTRUMENT FAULTS, both of which produced confident wrong answers:\n  * The peek reported HELD whenever `flock` exited non-zero -- conflating \"held\"\n    with \"file missing\" and any other failure. A zero and a missing input must\n    not look the same. Fixed by separating FREE / HELD / ERROR / NOFILE.\n  * The holder itself ended in `sleep 300`, a forked child inheriting the lock\n    fd -- so my \"no children\" control had a child, and the control arm and the\n    test arm were the same arm. Fixed with a bash builtin `read -t` on a fifo,\n    which forks nothing.\nBoth faults flattered the conclusion I was about to draw. The corrected runs\nshow a clean difference between A and B, which is what makes either mean\nanything."  # 78e1
- op: discuss  # 6250
