- op: discuss  # b72b
  at: '2026-09-15T22:45:48Z'  # b72b
  by: agent  # b72b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b72b
  clock: 8  # b72b
  nonce: b72b  # b72b
  message: "DONE in PR #977, merged manually by the owner (governance), cleaned up via\n`auto-pr gov-cleanup 977`. dev fast-forwarded to e8a4ffe050.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE, on the merged tree, re-running this item's own scenario.\n\n  A. THE ORIGINAL BUG'S SHAPE -- a leftover marker file, no holder, aged 10h,\n     which is exactly what WI-pohir observed:\n         hooks read it as: FREE  ->  self-healing stays LIVE\n         detector says:    nothing\n     Under the old flag semantics this same state silently disabled ops\n     self-healing. It is now inert. That is the fix, not a report of the fix.\n\n  B. THE ONE REMAINING WAY suppression can outlive its owner -- lock genuinely\n     held, recorded owner gone:\n         [tracker] WARNING: ops self-healing is suppressed by an ORPHANED lock.\n           .git/tracker-recover-disabled is locked, but the process that took\n           it (pid 4194304) is gone. A child it forked inherited the lock fd...\n           It clears itself when that child exits, which is normally seconds.\n           If this persists, find the orphan holding the lock:\n             lslocks | grep tracker-recover-disabled\n\ncore.hooksPath points at .githooks, so the merged hooks are live with no install\nstep -- checked rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. The marker is an fcntl lock; the OS releases it even on SIGKILL,\nso the FILE existing means nothing and only a live holder suppresses anything.\nHeld by auto-pr (three action entry points, via _autopr_hold_recover_lock) and by\ndo_sync (RecoverSuppressionLock, acquired before its try and released in the\nmatching finally). Both hooks test the lock, not the file. The lock body records\npid/started -- SyncGate's shape -- so the detector can ask whether the recorded\nowner is alive instead of starting a stopwatch.\n\nTHE RISK I FILED THIS ITEM WITH WAS REAL AND SHAPED THE DESIGN. A hook peeking\nwith a fresh open() sees the lock even as a DESCENDANT holding the inherited fd,\nso that concern is closed. But every git and curl auto-pr forks inherits the lock\nfd and bash has no close-on-exec escape -- `exec {var}>` does not set it, and\n/proc/<child>/fd still showed the marker. A naive conversion would therefore have\ntraded a loud 15-minute window for a SILENT PERMANENT one. Hence the body and the\norphan check.\n\nWHAT IT DELETED. _UNOWNED_GRACE_SECONDS, and the practice of measuring auto-pr's\nphases to pick a number -- including the 900s measured and shipped in PR #976\nhours earlier. A lock answers \"is anybody there\" as a fact. Deleting the constant\nis better than keeping one that must be re-measured whenever auto-pr's phases\nmove.\n\nACCEPTANCE MATRIX, against the REAL hooks and the REAL acquire function extracted\nfrom auto-pr rather than re-implemented:\n  no file / leftover file / holder killed  -> both hooks recover\n  live holder / orphan child holds lock    -> both hooks skip\n  orphan child exits                       -> both hooks recover\nHook suite 45 passed 0 failed, including two NEW difference arms asserting an\nUNHELD leftover file suppresses nothing -- the arms that fail against the defect.\nPython 4381 passed; tracker package 100% in isolation; ratchet 669 delta +0.\n\nMY OWN INSTRUMENT FAULTS, recorded because the wrong versions were the plausible\nones: the first peek reported HELD on ANY non-zero flock exit, conflating \"held\"\nwith \"file missing\"; and the first holder ended in `sleep`, a forked child\ninheriting the lock fd, so the \"no children\" control had a child and the control\nand test arms were the same arm. Both flattered the conclusion I was about to\ndraw. The matrix above is from the corrected harness."  # b72b
